- Retrieved practices:
- Avoid invalid pointer-to-member access in C++
WHAT: Using .* or ->* with a pointer-to-member that refers to a member not present in the dynamic type of the object, or using a null pointer-to-member, causes undefined behavior.
WHY: Undefined behavior can lead to memory corruption, crashes, or exploitable security vulnerabilities.
HOW: Ensure the dynamic type of the object actually defines the targeted member before using .* or ->*; never use null pointer-to-member values; avoid casting pointer-to-member values across unrelated types.
EXAMPLE: Before (unsafe): `void (Base::*pm)() = nullptr; (obj.*pm)();` After (safe): `void (Base::*pm)() = &Base::f; if (pm) (obj.*pm)();`

- Avoid reserved identifiers in C++
WHAT: Declaring or defining identifiers that begin with an underscore at file scope (for variables, functions, literals, etc.) can clash with implementation-reserved names, causing undefined behavior or linkage conflicts.
WHY: Name clashes with the standard library or runtime can lead to unpredictable behavior, hard-to-diagnose bugs, or security vulnerabilities due to calling or referencing the wrong symbol.
HOW: Do not start file-scope or global identifiers with an underscore; instead, use descriptive names that do not match implementation-reserved patterns, and ensure user-defined literals use non-reserved operator names (e.g., operator"" _x, not _x).
EXAMPLE (C++ before): `static const std::size_t _max_limit = 1024; std::size_t _limit = 100;`
EXAMPLE (C++ after): `static const std::size_t max_limit = 1024; std::size_t limit = 100;`

- Do not access freed memory
WHAT: Accessing memory after it has been freed or gone out of scope (dangling pointers) leads to undefined behavior and potential security vulnerabilities.
WHY: Use-after-free can cause crashes, data corruption, or be exploited for arbitrary code execution.
HOW: Ensure object lifetimes cover all uses; prefer RAII types like std::unique_ptr or std::string over raw heap buffers, and avoid manual delete/free where possible.
EXAMPLE: Before (unsafe): `char *buff = new char[32]; delete[] buff; std::cout << buff;` After (safe): `std::string str = argv[1]; std::cout << str;`

- Do not access freed memory
WHAT: Accessing memory after it has been freed (including double free or use-after-free) causes undefined behavior and can corrupt program state.
WHY: Attackers can exploit use-after-free or double-free bugs to execute arbitrary code, escalate privileges, or crash the application.
HOW: Set pointers to null immediately after freeing, avoid reusing freed pointers, ensure each allocation is freed exactly once, and use tools (ASan, Valgrind) to detect invalid frees and accesses.
EXAMPLE (C++ - before): `delete p; foo(*p);`
